  "CIO_paragraphs": [
    "The unchanged balanced score keeps ERO first, with HBM moving from fifth to second, followed by LUNMF, SCCO and GMBXF. ERO's vendor FCF yield rises from 1.6% to 4.3%; HBM's falls from 4.7% to 2.9% despite a much stronger growth input. A higher rank therefore needs a cash-conversion check, not just a growth story.",
    "Current issuer releases support real operating progress but also important countercases. Ero reported stronger operating cash generation alongside higher copper-cost guidance. Hudbay reported strong free cash flow, while regional cost pressure and project-dedicated cash limit a simple balance-sheet reading. Lundin Mining generated cash but cut production guidance after weather disruption.",
    "COPX fell 2.84% in observed adjusted prices from November 6 to 13. Relative issuer appeal does not protect against a falling copper market. All 17 scored names have November 13 price observations; CSFFF remains unavailable. RIO and ZIJMF market caps reconcile to vendor implied-share totals, but legal group/share-class scope is not independently proved. No cap or share count was substituted."
  ],
  "relative_selection_qualification": "A long/short issuer basket is a relative-selection idea, not a neutral gold or copper exposure by construction. Differences in commodity mix, operating leverage, geography, FX, project spending and borrowing costs can dominate it. The historical PM recipe is not rebalanced here; no new long/short positions, weights, borrow assumptions or performance claims are created.",
